## Codebase Patterns

- `WebComponent` (from `@substrate-system/web-component`) uses
  `this.TAG` (an **instance** field) inside `emit()`/`on()`/`off()`,
  not the static `TAG`. Any subclass that only sets `static TAG = '...'`
  without also setting an instance field `TAG = '...'` will silently
  emit events namespaced as `:eventname` (empty prefix) instead of
  `tag-name:eventname`, and consumer listeners for the namespaced event
  will never fire. Always set both:
  ```ts
  export class Foo extends WebComponent {
      static TAG = 'foo'
      TAG = Foo.TAG
      ...
  }
  ```
  This was a latent bug in the original `image-input` scaffold (only
  `static TAG` was set) that had gone unnoticed because no test
  exercised a namespaced custom event before this session.
- Reflected attributes (`static reflectedStringAttributes` /
  `reflectedBooleanAttributes`) get auto-generated getters/setters by
  the base class's `define()`. Adding a name here is enough to get
  `this.foo` / `this.foo = x` working; you still write your own
  `handleChange_foo(oldValue, newValue)` for DOM side effects, which
  runs on `attributeChangedCallback`, not on `render()`. `render()`
  only runs once (from `connectedCallback`), so any post-mount state
  changes (alt text, preview image, overlay toggling) should mutate
  the existing DOM directly (via `this.qs(...)`) rather than
  re-invoking `render()`, or you will lose imperative state like the
  `<img>` `src` set from a file/object URL.
- CSS: `src/_vars.css` holds `:root` custom properties, imported by
  `src/index.css` via `@import './_vars.css'`. The `build-css` /
  `build-css:min` npm scripts now pass `--bundle` to `lightningcss` so
  the import is inlined into `dist/index.css` (bundler CLI does not
  resolve `@import` by default, only with `--bundle`). Keep this flag
  if more CSS partials are added later (e.g. for `crop.ts`).
- Preview images use `URL.createObjectURL(file)` (not `FileReader`
  data URLs) so the same object-URL lifecycle/revocation pattern can
  be reused by `image-crop` later. Always revoke the previous object
  URL before creating a new one and on remove, to avoid leaking blobs.
- Test pattern for simulating a file input: build a `DataTransfer`,
  `dataTransfer.items.add(file)`, assign `input.files =
  dataTransfer.files`, then `input.dispatchEvent(new Event('change',
  { bubbles: true }))`. See `test/index.ts` `selectFile()` helper.
- No "dev-browser" skill is registered in this harness. Use the
  Playwright MCP tools directly against `npm run start` (vite dev
  server) for the "verify in browser" acceptance criteria, and kill
  the dev server + delete the `.playwright-mcp/` scratch directory
  afterwards (it is not gitignored and holds screenshots/snapshots
  that shouldn't be committed).

## 2026-07-23 - US-001

- Implemented the preview overlay for `image-input`: after a file is
  selected, the component shows the image preview with an overlay
  containing an "+ ALT"/"ALT" badge button, a pencil edit button, and
  an x remove button, matching the Bluesky-style thumbnail chrome.
- Added a minimal `alt` reflected string attribute/property (full
  scope belongs to US-003, but US-001's acceptance criteria requires
  the badge to switch state once alt text is set via the `alt`
  property, so the property and the badge-toggling `handleChange_alt`
  were added now). Setting `el.alt` updates the preview `<img>`'s
  `alt` attribute and toggles a `.has-alt` class on the badge (tests
  assert on the class, not the button's text content, per the
  no-brittle-text-assertions rule).
- Clicking remove clears the file, revokes the preview object URL,
  clears the alt attribute, resets the file input's value, hides the
  overlay, and emits `image-input:remove`.
- Switched the preview from `FileReader`/data-URL to
  `URL.createObjectURL(file)`, revoked on remove and before setting a
  new file, per the PRD's Technical Considerations (also needed later
  for US-007's leak-avoidance requirement).
- Found and fixed a pre-existing bug in `ImageInput`: only `static TAG`
  was set, so `this.emit(...)` (which reads the instance field
  `this.TAG` from the base class) produced malformed event names like
  `:remove` instead of `image-input:remove`, and consumer listeners
  never fired. Added `TAG = ImageInput.TAG` as an instance field. See
  Codebase Patterns above.
- Added `src/_vars.css` (new file) with the CSS custom properties for
  the overlay (pill colors, radius, sizes, focus color), imported from
  `src/index.css` via `@import`. Updated `build-css` / `build-css:min`
  npm scripts to pass `--bundle` to `lightningcss` so the import
  resolves in the shipped `dist/index.css`.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `src/index.css`, `src/_vars.css`
  (new), `test/index.ts`, `package.json` (build-css scripts),
  `specs/prd.json` (US-001 passes: true).
- Tests: added 3 new tape tests (overlay controls presence + a11y
  names, alt property toggling the badge class, remove clearing state
  and emitting the event) plus kept the original smoke test. All 15
  assertions pass via `npm test` (lint + typecheck + build + tape-run
  in a real browser via `tape-run`).
- Verified manually in the browser with the Playwright MCP tools
  against `npm run start`: uploaded a real file via the file chooser,
  confirmed the overlay (ALT badge, pencil, x) renders with correct
  accessible names, clicked remove and confirmed the component returns
  to the empty state, and confirmed `image-input:change` fires with
  the correct namespaced type and `{ file }` detail (this last check
  also validates the `TAG` fix works outside the test harness).
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - See Codebase Patterns section above (TAG instance field gotcha,
    render()-runs-once pattern, lightningcss --bundle, object URL
    lifecycle, DataTransfer test helper, no dev-browser skill here).
  - Icons are inline `<svg>` with `currentColor` stroke (no icon
    library dependency, consistent with the "zero new runtime
    dependencies" project goal). Reuse this approach for any future
    icon needs (e.g. crop handles).
- Playwright MCP tools ARE available in this harness (the earlier
  "no dev-browser skill" note still holds -- there's no dedicated
  skill -- but the `mcp__plugin_ed3d-playwright_...` tool set works
  fine against `npm run start`). Use `browser_navigate`,
  `browser_file_upload` after clicking the file input to trigger the
  chooser, and `browser_evaluate` to attach listeners / read captured
  event details, since accessibility snapshots don't show custom
  event activity.

## 2026-07-23 - US-002

- Implemented edit/alt-text request events: clicking the pencil
  (`.edit`) button emits `image-input:edit` with `{ file }`; clicking
  the ALT badge (`.alt-badge`) emits `image-input:alt` with
  `{ file, alt }` (`alt` is the current `this.alt` value, or `''` if
  unset). Both handlers are no-ops if no file has been selected yet
  (buttons are only visible once `.has-image` is set, but this guards
  against programmatic clicks). Neither handler performs navigation or
  opens a dialog -- that responsibility is left entirely to the
  consumer per the PRD's headless-pattern intent (US-008 will wire a
  `<dialog>` in the example app).
- Events bubble by default via the base `WebComponent.emit()` (default
  `{ bubbles: true }`), confirmed both by a unit test listening on
  `document.body` and by the browser verification below.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts` (added `handleEdit`/`handleAlt`
  bound methods, wired up in `setupEventListeners`/
  `disconnectedCallback`), `test/index.ts` (4 new tests: edit event +
  bubbling, alt event with empty and then set alt text, no-dialog
  guard), `specs/prd.json` (US-002 `passes: true`).
- Tests: 4 new tape tests, all pass; full `npm test` (lint + build +
  typecheck via `tsc --emitDeclarationOnly` + tape-run) passes with 22
  total assertions.
- Verified manually in the browser with the Playwright MCP tools
  against `npm run start`: uploaded a real 1x1 PNG via the file
  chooser, attached `image-input:edit`/`image-input:alt` listeners via
  `browser_evaluate`, clicked the edit and ALT badge buttons, and
  confirmed both events fired with the correct file name and alt text
  in `detail`, and that no `<dialog>` appeared. Killed the vite dev
  server and removed `.playwright-mcp/` afterwards.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - See Codebase Patterns section above (new note: Playwright MCP
    tools work fine here despite no dedicated dev-browser skill).
  - `handleEdit`/`handleAlt` both read `this.#file`/`this.alt`
    directly rather than threading state through render() -- this is
    the reason the "render()-runs-once, mutate DOM imperatively"
    pattern in Codebase Patterns exists; keep following it for US-003
    onward instead of re-rendering.
